    "summary": "High‑energy Boston show with epic Smokestack Lightning and tight jams",
    "blurb": "Features Pigpen‑fronted first set highlighted by a scorching Smokestack Lightning and Keith’s piano fills. The second set rolls through Sugar Magnolia, Uncle John's Band and a seamless Not Fade Away > Turn On Your Lovelight medley, maintaining a high‑energy flow throughout.",
    "review": "December 2, 1971 at Boston Music Hall finds the Dead in top form, delivering a performance that feels both loose and razor‑sharp. The first set is Pigpen‑centric, launching into an explosive Smokestack Lightning that still crackles on today’s tapes, while Keith Godchaux’s piano adds bright, jazzy color to every turn. Jerry’s guitar sits front‑and‑center—punchy on Bertha and Casey Jones—and the rhythm section locks in with a buttery bass from Phil and a tight drum groove from Bill, especially on the funk‑infused Love Light jam. The second set shifts into classic summer vibes with Sugar Magnolia and Uncle John's Band before diving into a seamless Not Fade Away > Turn On Your Lovelight > Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad sequence that keeps the crowd rolling. The encore’s Johnny B. Goode caps a night that remains one of ’71’s most vivid snapshots of the band’s evolving sound.",
